\section{A Resolution Step Is Not a Search Strategy}

In selective linear definite-clause resolution (SLD resolution), start with a list of goals and select one. Rename the variables of a candidate clause to fresh ones, unify its head with the selected goal, then replace the goal with the clause body. Apply that unifier to the \emph{whole} resulting goal list. With a fact the body is empty, so the selected goal simply disappears. Reaching the empty list yields a proof. The computed answer composes the substitutions along the successful branch and projects them onto the original query variables.

The same goal-list transformations occur even if the implementation represents them as stack frames and variable cells. Van Emden and Kowalski's account of procedure invocation by resolution supplies the distinction between a proof step and the resulting proof. Prolog normally selects goals left to right, considers matching clauses in source order, and explores each branch depth first. Luger and Stubblefield's Prolog search examples make the consequence clear: when an attempted conjunction fails, a retry must restore the bindings made after the choice being retried. A failure on one branch is not a proof that every branch fails.

\section{An Executable Dependency Query}

Read \verb|dep(X, Y)| as ``the toy compiler stage \texttt{X} directly needs \texttt{Y}.'' The recursive relation describes paths with at least one edge.

\begin{verbatim}
dep(obj, asm).
dep(obj, dbg).
dep(asm, ir).
dep(dbg, ir).

depends(X, Y) :- dep(X, Y).
depends(X, Y) :- dep(X, Z), depends(Z, Y).
\end{verbatim}

Query \verb|?- depends(obj, Y).| and request more answers after the first proof. The ordinary order is \texttt{asm}, \texttt{dbg}, \texttt{ir}, \texttt{ir}. The two copies of \texttt{ir} describe paths through different intermediate stages. To see why substitutions must propagate past the first subgoal, query \verb|?- depends(obj, Y), dep(Y, ir).|. Each proposed \texttt{Y} is now the first argument of the second goal. The direct answers survive; the recursive proofs with \verb|Y = ir| do not, since there is no outgoing edge from \texttt{ir}.

Freshening a clause is essential even when the recursive call uses the same textual predicate and variable names. In the outer recursive clause the local intermediate stage can be \texttt{asm}; on a subsequent recursive call its intermediate stage can independently be \texttt{ir}. Reusing one storage cell for these two activations would accidentally require the different stages to coincide. Implementations with environments obtain fresh local variables by allocating or safely reusing slots, not by generating printable names for them.

