\section{Walkthrough: Follow a Branch, Then Unwind It}

This reference interpreter uses immutable substitutions, so a failed branch is undone by discarding its local version. A destructive implementation needs explicit rollback instead.

\begin{verbatim}
solve(goals, bindings):
    if goals is empty:
        yield project(bindings, original_query_variables)
        return
    selected, rest := first(goals), remaining(goals)
    for rule in matching_predicate_clauses_in_source_order:
        head, body := fresh_copy(rule)
        next := try_unify(selected, head, bindings)
        if next succeeds:
            yield all from solve(body ++ rest, next)
            # retry with bindings, not next
\end{verbatim}

Here the list stores unexpanded terms; each lookup dereferences them under the current bindings. The invariant is that the pending goals under those bindings are the outstanding obligations along the current proof prefix. In the second query, the direct clause first produces \verb|Y = asm|; the final goal is then \verb|dep(asm, ir)|, which succeeds. Retrying produces \verb|Y = dbg| from the same direct clause. On a recursive branch the first \texttt{dep} can bind the fresh \texttt{Z} to \texttt{asm}, and the recursive call binds \texttt{Y} to \texttt{ir}. The last obligation becomes \verb|dep(ir, ir)| and fails. At no point may the failed \verb|Y = ir| binding leak into the alternative through \texttt{dbg}. \Cref{fig:visual-340} shows why the shorter proofs are encountered first.

