\section{A Proof May Exist Beyond an Infinite Branch}

Add this alternative formulation to the same Prolog file:

\begin{verbatim}
late_depends(X, Y) :- late_depends(X, Z), dep(Z, Y).
late_depends(X, Y) :- dep(X, Y).
\end{verbatim}

This is also the nonempty transitive closure, now extending a path at its last edge. Nevertheless \verb|?- late_depends(obj, Y).| keeps selecting a recursive call before it checks any \texttt{dep} goal. Even an acyclic input graph cannot prevent this divergence; the graph is never consulted. The proof using the fact for \verb|dep(obj, asm)| exists but standard depth-first traversal never reaches it.

Fair exploration can avoid starving finite proofs, but does not in general turn a nonterminating search into finite failure. Another option is tabling: add \verb|:- table late_depends/2.| before the two clauses in SWI-Prolog. Repeated variant calls, equal up to variable renaming, share answers instead of endlessly expanding the same subgoal. For this finite program the table reaches completion and the distinct values are \texttt{asm}, \texttt{dbg}, and \texttt{ir}, without promising their order. Swift and Warren describe the role of tabling in XSB \cite{swift2012xsb}. On a program with unbounded term construction, tabling alone does not guarantee a finite set of calls or answers.

A practical test therefore separates finding a first answer from exhausting all answers. Moving the direct clause first in \texttt{late\_depends/2} gives immediate direct results; it does not remove the left-recursive search. A test that stops as soon as it sees \texttt{asm} misses this difference. Test the expected answer set under an evaluator known to complete, and use a separate resource budget when checking the operational behavior of the untabled implementation.

For a compiler dependency pass, this is an output requirement rather than a presentation detail: an incomplete traversal must not be cached as a complete dependency set. Select tabling, a bottom-up closure, or an explicitly bounded query before handing the result to an invalidator or build scheduler.
